{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe outbound Jira comment dedup key is one composition of functions in the differ; the\nsend is a *different* composition in the adapter. Every convergence bug in this area has\nbeen a point where the two drifted.\n\nToday, at `_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_comments.py:591-594`, the differ builds its\nkey as `_normalize_comment_body(codec.to_wire(_decorate_outbound_comment(\nsanitizer.fit_comment(body))))`, but the mutation it appends carries only\n`_decorate_outbound_comment(body)` — the **un-fitted** body. The transport then re-derives\nthe wire independently: Cloud at `adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py:531`\n(`json.dumps(codec.to_wire(fit_preserving_marker(body, codec.fit_outbound)))`), Data Center\nat `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:543` (`transport.add_comment(remote_id,\nsanitizer.sanitize_comment(body))`, whose truncate is `fit_preserving_marker(text,\nself._fit_raw)`).\n\nSo the key **PREDICTS** the wire the send path will produce rather than being taken from\nthe bytes actually sent, and it is correct only for as long as three separately-maintained\ncompositions stay in lockstep. `fit_comment_as_sent`\n(`outbound_comments.py:290`) exists solely to re-run the send's fit, and each backend's\n`fit_comment` (`adapters/jira/backend.py:156`, `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:309`)\ndocuments that it must reproduce its adapter's send composition exactly. That is the\nstructure this leaf removes.\n\n(The normalisation asymmetry at the same site is already closed by bug\n`17c3-2f6e-a5e0-438c`; it is not what this leaf targets.)\n\n## Approach\nAdd to `outbound_comments.py` (the backend-neutral core — it names no vendor and no\nnumeric limit, pinned by `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_neutrality.py`):\n\n```\nFittedComment = frozen dataclass(landed_text: str, wire_body: str)\n\nfit_for_send(body, sanitizer, codec, *, inbound_mapper=None) -> FittedComment\n    wire_body   = _decorate_outbound_comment(sanitizer.fit_comment(body))\n    landed_text = _normalize_comment_body(codec.to_wire(wire_body),\n                                          inbound_mapper=inbound_mapper)\n```\n\n`_diff_comments` calls it once per candidate body: it keys on `landed_text` (byte-identical\nto today's `compare_body`, so the diff decision is unchanged) and puts `wire_body` on the\nmutation entry alongside `body`. Both enactment consumers then send `wire_body`.\n\nThe key is therefore no longer a prediction: it is the decoded readback of the exact string\nhanded to the transport. `fit_comment` and its two implementers stop being a convergence\nobligation and become an ordinary fit, because nothing downstream re-derives the text.\n\n## Constraint: BOTH mutation-enactment consumers are in Scope\nTwo structurally identical consumers, not one — same `entry.get(\"body\", \"\")` read, same\nsingle-attempt `client.add_comment(...)`, same `_record_comment_id` follow-up:\n- `_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py:368-379` (create path);\n- `_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_apply_phases.py:377-386` (update path).\n\nScoping only one would leave the other sending the un-fitted `body` while the key came\nfrom `wire_body` — the exact drift this leaf closes, on half the traffic. Both read\n`entry.get(\"wire_body\") or entry.get(\"body\", \"\")`, so a mutation entry queued **before**\nthe cutover (no `wire_body`) still sends.\n\n`dispatch_one.py:636` — the illegal-transition status fallback — posts a short literal\n(`local status changed to <status>`) that never passes through the differ and has no\n`wire_body`. It is deliberately OUT of scope and keeps reaching the adapter's own fit.\n\n## Constraint: the adapter's fit stays, and must be IDEMPOTENT\nThe send path is not made encode-free: Cloud's wire is JSON-serialised ADF, so\n`codec.to_wire` must run at send time. What must not happen is a fit that **changes the\nbytes** after the key was taken. The adapters keep their defensive fit (it still guards\ndirect callers like `dispatch_one.py:636`), and the property that makes the key sound is\nthat re-fitting an already-fitted body returns it **byte-identical** — `fit_preserving_marker`\ntruncates content and re-appends the marker, so a within-limit input is returned unchanged.\nThat idempotence is asserted directly, on both the Cloud and DC fitters, rather than assumed.\n\n## Constraint: the `fit_comment` port keeps its contract\n`_engine/rebar_reconciler/_backend.py:487` declares `fit_comment(self, body: str) -> str`.\n`fit_comment_as_sent` is **RETAINED** unchanged as the shared implementation, so the\nProtocol signature and both implementers keep working. Only the differ moves to\n`fit_for_send`, confining the change to the dedup-key path.\n\n## Out of scope: the truncation-suffix literal\n`\" … [truncated by reconciler]\"` is defined three times\n(`adapters/jira/comment_limits.py:46`, `adapters/jira/adf.py:333`,\n`adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py:69`). That is a real duplication, but a *deliberate* one:\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_rich_text_codec.py:69-71` records the reason — \"the shared\nlayer must not import the Cloud module\" — and pins the three to one value. Collapsing it is a\nseparate decision about package layering, not part of deriving the key from the sent bytes,\nand is left to its own ticket. The `32767` occurrences under `src/rebar` are likewise\nuntouched: they are sourced product knowledge (the DC `jira.text.field.character.limit`\ndefault, cited to JRASERVER-28519), not duplication.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_comments.py`,\n`_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py`,\n`_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_apply_phases.py`,\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_fit_for_send.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nHappy path over the captured corpus `tests/fixtures/cloud_adf_corpus/bodies_*.json`: for\nevery body, `fit_for_send(...).landed_text` equals the marker-stripped decode of\n`codec.to_wire(wire_body)`. Edge: an over-limit body keeps `RECONCILER_MARKER` and the key\nstill matches; a DC body fits at `comment_max_chars()`. Regression pinning (must pass\nunchanged): `test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py`,\n`mutate/test_dc_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py`,\n`mutate/test_outbound_comment_marker_survives_truncation.py`,\n`test_acli_comment_adf_encode.py`, `test_rich_text_codec.py`, `test_backend_neutrality.py`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The dedup key is the readback of the bytes sent: a test fits an over-long comment,\n      drives it through a fake transport, and asserts\n      `key == landed_text == strip_marker(decode(what the transport received))`.\n- [ ] For every corpus body, `landed_text` equals the marker-stripped decode of\n      `codec.to_wire(wire_body)`; checked by the new corpus test.\n- [ ] `landed_text` is byte-identical to the key `_diff_comments` produces today, so the\n      diff decision is unchanged; checked by the pinning suites passing unchanged.\n- [ ] BOTH enactment consumers (`dispatch_one.py` create, `dispatch_apply_phases.py` update)\n      hand the transport `entry[\"wire_body\"]`; checked by a test driving each path through a\n      fake transport and comparing against `fit_for_send(...).wire_body`.\n- [ ] Re-fitting `wire_body` returns it byte-identical on BOTH the Cloud and the DC fitter,\n      so the adapter's defensive fit cannot move the bytes after the key was taken; checked\n      by an idempotence test over in-limit and over-limit bodies.\n- [ ] A mutation entry written before the cutover (no `wire_body`) still sends its `body`,\n      on BOTH paths; checked by a back-compat test.\n- [ ] `fit_comment_as_sent` and the `_backend.fit_comment` Protocol keep their `-> str`\n      contract; checked by both implementers (`adapters/jira/backend.py:156`,\n      `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:309`) passing unchanged."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787376257629778001,"uuid":"bc5bcb0b-a3a2-4458-a6da-34ecf29d4d26"}